<p>Brazil bought USD 25.2 million of Indian dehydrated onion in calendar 2024, making it India's second-largest market for this product, just behind the United States, out of a USD 221.4 million worldwide total. Most of that demand comes from Brazil's large food-processing sector. Dehydrated onion keeps for a long time, needs no cold chain and holds a consistent specification, so it suits industrial production better than buying fresh onion season by season. This guide is for Brazilian importers and food manufacturers setting up or reviewing an Indian supply line. It covers how to specify form and mesh, what the Santos route asks of packaging, which documents sit on each side, and where first orders tend to go wrong.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p>Dehydrated onion is one of the most widely spread Indian agricultural exports in our research. About 120 destination markets shared the 2024 total, and no single market came close to dominating it. The United States leads at USD 25.7 million, Brazil follows closely at USD 25.2 million, and Germany, Indonesia, Belgium, the UK, Russia, the Netherlands, Spain and Canada make up the rest of the top ten.</p>

<!-- insight:buyer-segmentation -->
<h2>Why does Brazil buy the most tonnage but pay less per kilo than the US?</h2>
<p>By volume Brazil was the largest market in the 2024 table: about 13,631 MT, against about 11,461 MT for the United States. Dividing the WITS/UN Comtrade values by tonnage puts Brazil at about USD 1.85 per kg, against USD 2.25 for the United States, USD 2.04 for Germany, USD 1.35 for Indonesia and USD 1.96 for India's exports as a whole. Had Brazil's tonnage been bought at the US average, it would have been worth about USD 30.6 million instead of USD 25.2 million.</p>
<p>A unit value averages every form and grade shipped, so it is not a price, but the gap suggests Brazil's mix leans further towards bulk industrial lots than the US or German trade, where more fine powder and tightly specified retail-grade material may be included. Scenario: a São Paulo seasoning blender buying 80-100 mesh powder and a meat processor buying kibbled onion in jumbo bags are both "Brazil buyers", and the blender should expect to pay above the Brazil average while the processor's offer sits near or below it. Compare quotes against your own form and mesh, not against the country average.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>Why dehydrated onion avoids fresh onion's policy swings</h2>
<p>If you have looked at Indian onion before and been put off by policy volatility, the short version is that dehydrated onion sits under a different customs heading. Fresh onion (HS 0703.10) has been subject to a Minimum Export Price, to export duty at various points, and to a full export ban in 2023-24. India uses all of these to manage domestic onion prices. Dehydrated onion (HS 0712.20) has not had the same restriction history, so it is a more predictable product for a recurring sourcing programme. It is not completely insulated, though. As the price section below explains, fresh-onion policy still reaches dehydrated onion through the cost of raw onion.</p>

<!-- insight:spec-to-application -->
<h2>Which specification should a Brazilian food manufacturer write into the contract?</h2>
<p>For a Brazilian food-manufacturing buyer the specification comes down to five parameters. Each one should be written into the contract, not left to the supplier's standard sheet:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong>: kibbled/flakes, chopped, minced, granules or powder. See the comparison table at the end of this guide for typical sizes and uses.</li>
  <li><strong>Particle size / mesh</strong>: for granules and powder, give a sieve range with a percentage passing and retained. A trade name alone is not enough, because it is the distribution that decides how the material flows through dosing equipment and disperses in a blend.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong>: typically ≤5-6%. Dehydrated onion absorbs moisture from the air, so this limit matters at arrival as well as at dispatch.</li>
  <li><strong>Colour</strong>: white or light-golden, from a white or red onion base. Heat damage during drying shows up as browning, so agree a colour standard or reference sample.</li>
  <li><strong>Microbiological panel</strong>: standard plate count, yeast and mould, and salmonella/E. coli limits. These matter most when the onion goes into formulated products that receive no further kill step.</li>
</ul>
<p>Foreign matter is normally held at ≤0.5%. If your production line runs automated dosing, tell us, so that the particle-size tolerance can be tightened to suit it.</p>
<p>Scenario: a Brazilian maker of instant soup sachets doses minced onion through a volumetric feeder and adds it after the cooking step. Its contract should state the 1-3 mm sieve range with a maximum share of fines, moisture of 5% or below at dispatch, and a total plate count, yeast and mould, and salmonella limit set for a product with no further heat step. A contract that only says "minced, white onion, moisture ≤6%" lets through a lot that bridges in the feeder and is tested against our default panel instead of yours.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>How should dehydrated onion be packed for the long voyage to Santos?</h2>
<p>Dehydrated onion ships in a standard dry container with no reefer, and it has an 18-24 month shelf life in cool, dry, sealed storage. The difficult part of the Brazil lane is the 30-40 day ocean transit, not temperature. Packs are 20kg or 25kg multi-wall paper bags with a PP or poly liner, vacuum-sealed options where you need longer shelf life, or 1 MT jumbo bags for bulk industrial buyers. Kibbled and chopped forms are bulky, so a container fills by volume before it reaches its weight limit: expect roughly 6-7 MT of kibbled or chopped in a 20ft and 14-16 MT in a 40ft, while denser powder and granules reach about 12-14 MT and 24-25 MT. The inner liner is the part that protects the product through a long, humid sea voyage, so treat it as a specification item rather than packaging detail.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<!-- insight:documentation-failures -->
<p>On the Brazilian side, the importer must be authorised on SISCOMEX with a RADAR licence before the first shipment. This registration is entirely the importer's responsibility and is a common cause of first-order delay. MAPA regulates imports of plant-origin products, so the phytosanitary certificate and the product description on the invoice need to match the consignment exactly. Bags destined for Brazilian retail also need Portuguese-language labelling under ANVISA's food-labelling rules. For industrial bulk bags, agree the bag markings with your importer: product name, lot number, production and best-before dates. Share your compliance checklist and we will confirm the documentation for your shipment.</p>
<p>Scenario: a first-time importer confirms the order, we dispatch within 12-18 days, and the vessel takes 30-40 days to Santos. The RADAR application was started the week the order was signed and its approved value limit is below the invoice. The container lands about six to eight weeks after the order and then waits at the terminal, accruing storage and demurrage, until the authorisation is fixed. Starting RADAR before the purchase order, and checking that its limit covers the invoice value, removes the most common delay on this lane.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What moves dehydrated onion pricing</h2>
<p>Dehydrated onion is not subject to the fresh-onion Minimum Export Price, but its biggest cost input is still fresh onion. Several independent factors move the price you are quoted:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Raw-onion cost.</strong> India's dehydration industry is concentrated in Gujarat, around Mahuva and the wider Bhavnagar belt. It processes mainly white onion bought during the harvest campaign and dried in season. A short or weather-damaged crop raises the cost of raw onion, which means more money per kilogram of finished product, because several kilograms of fresh onion go into each kilogram of dehydrated onion.</li>
  <li><strong>Fresh-onion policy.</strong> MEP changes, export duties or bans on fresh onion shift domestic onion prices. Those shifts reach dehydrated onion through raw-material cost, even though the dehydrated product itself is not restricted.</li>
  <li><strong>Timing within the season.</strong> Contracts placed during or just after the processing campaign are priced on known crop costs. Later in the year the price depends on how much stock processors are holding and what they expect from the next crop.</li>
  <li><strong>Form and mesh.</strong> Powder and fine granules need extra milling and sieving and lose more yield than kibbled or chopped, and a tighter particle-size band costs more to hold.</li>
  <li><strong>Freight and currency.</strong> Container freight from India to Santos is a long-haul lane and swings with global container rates. INR/USD also moves the FOB price.</li>
</ul>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your form, mesh, microbiological panel, packaging and destination port, never as a generic per-tonne rate. For a fast, accurate quote, send us the form, the mesh or size range, the microbiological limits, the pack type, the quantity and the delivery window.</p>
