<!-- insight:rejection-patterns -->
<h2>What goes wrong on a first Brazil order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p><strong>Importer registration left too late.</strong> The most common first-order delay has nothing to do with the product. If the importer's SISCOMEX/RADAR authorisation is not active, or its limits do not cover the order value, the cargo can reach Santos and sit there accruing storage and demurrage while the paperwork catches up. Confirm the registration before we book the container, not after the vessel sails. Also check with your customs broker whether MAPA needs anything specific in the phytosanitary certificate's additional declarations for this product.</p>
<p><strong>Microbiological results that do not match.</strong> Buyers often send a panel of limits without the test method or sampling plan. Our pre-shipment lab and your receiving lab can then legitimately report different counts. Agree the panel (total plate count, yeast and mould, coliforms/E. coli, salmonella), the method and the sampling basis in the purchase order. If the onion goes into products with no further heat treatment, say so up front so the limits can be set to match.</p>
<p><strong>Moisture picked up in transit.</strong> Dehydrated onion is hygroscopic. A 30-40 day voyage that crosses the equator exposes cargo to container condensation, and the "container rain" that damp, lumped product on arrival usually comes from it. The safeguards are intact inner liners that are heat-sealed or tightly closed, desiccant or kraft lining inside the container where appropriate, dry pallets or floor protection, and no loading of warm product. When you inspect on arrival, check the top layers and the bags near the doors first.</p>
<p><strong>Mesh or size mismatch.</strong> "Granules" and "minced" mean slightly different sizes to different processors. A buyer who orders by name alone can receive material that bridges in a hopper or leaves visible specks where fine dispersion was needed. Specify a sieve range and a tolerance, and approve a pre-shipment sample against it.</p>
<p><strong>Labelling and marking gaps.</strong> Bags with missing lot numbers or dates, or product descriptions that differ between bag, invoice and phytosanitary certificate, slow clearance and complicate your own traceability. Agree the bag markings before production starts.</p>
<p>Scenario: a lot packed at 5% moisture leaves a Gujarat port in a dry 20ft, crosses the equator and spends 30-40 days at sea. On arrival at Santos, the bags by the doors and on the top layer show lumped onion and test above the 6% contract limit, while the core of the load is within specification. The claim is settled by which bags were sampled, so agree the sampling plan before loading and photograph the stow, the liners and the container lining at the door.</p>
